1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,700
The 1950s, a shocking epidemic sweeps through America,

2
00:00:05,701 --> 00:00:08,240
crippling and killing young children.

3
00:00:08,241 --> 00:00:10,140
Thousands die.

4
00:00:10,141 --> 00:00:12,240
Many more are left helpless.

5
00:00:12,241 --> 00:00:16,240
The search for a cure will lead scientists to frantic trial,

6
00:00:16,241 --> 00:00:20,560
fatal error, and acts of scientific desperation.

7
00:00:20,561 --> 00:00:24,000
It will also transform an unknown young physician

8
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:26,400
into an international hero.

9
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:30,600
Now, the quest for the polio vaccine on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:46,520
As America entered the 1950s, a disease unheard of just 50

11
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:49,920
years earlier had grown into an annual event of terror

12
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,500
and apprehension.

13
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,400
Polio targeted anyone at any age, but the disease

14
00:00:56,401 --> 00:00:58,300
it targeted young, healthy children,

15
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,800
making it seem more destructive and cruel

16
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,200
than other diseases.

17
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:07,740
Polio, also called infantile paralysis, was a mystery.

18
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:12,140
Did they get it from the water?

19
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:13,620
Did they get it from insects?

20
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:15,520
Did they get it from animals?

21
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:18,060
We were petrified, and many parents, many of us,

22
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:19,860
kept our kids home.

23
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:21,460
We were frightened.

24
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:22,420
We were frightened.

25
00:01:22,421 --> 00:01:25,920
We were frightened.

26
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:27,920
The lives of millions of children

27
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:31,160
were changed by the fear polio caused.

28
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:33,240
It was really frightening for parents

29
00:01:33,340 --> 00:01:35,360
during the polio years.

30
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:38,040
There were terms such as polio weather.

31
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:39,800
Don't send the children to the beach.

32
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:42,140
They can't go to the community pool.

33
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:44,680
No one knew where polio came from.

34
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,520
They didn't know how to protect their children.

35
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:50,480
One day, scientists would learn that polio

36
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:52,640
was a fecal-oral disease.

37
00:01:52,641 --> 00:01:55,040
Children or adults coming in close contact

38
00:01:55,041 --> 00:01:58,080
could infect each other through water or food,

39
00:01:58,081 --> 00:02:00,680
contaminated by feces.

40
00:02:00,681 --> 00:02:02,320
But for the first half of the century,

41
00:02:02,321 --> 00:02:05,100
only the fearsome symptoms were understood.

42
00:02:05,101 --> 00:02:07,640
It began with a sniffle, then a fever,

43
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:10,780
then suddenly the cries of a child screaming in fear,

44
00:02:10,781 --> 00:02:15,580
unable to move, sometimes unable to get a breath of air.

45
00:02:15,581 --> 00:02:18,280
Polio patients spent weeks in the hospital,

46
00:02:18,281 --> 00:02:21,180
to battle the odds and recover.

47
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,760
The diagnosis of polio is just the beginning

48
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:27,360
of a long, painful journey.

49
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:30,920
The virus itself lasted anywhere from two to four weeks,

50
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:33,900
but the damage to the spine could shut down healthy muscles

51
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,340
in arms, legs, even lungs.

52
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,700
Therapy began immediately after the fever subsided.

53
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:47,240
Rehabilitation was long, tedious, and painful.

54
00:02:47,241 --> 00:02:51,040
One of the things that's not in a lot of the literature also

55
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:57,960
is that polio has a lot of pain associated with it

56
00:02:58,060 --> 00:02:59,460
on a daily basis.

57
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:03,960
Not a severe pain, but constant pain.

58
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:09,360
And the pain is associated not so much with the handicap,

59
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:12,200
but with the wearing of a brace.

60
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:15,660
With the help of a brace, the fortunate might walk again.

61
00:03:15,661 --> 00:03:18,360
The less fortunate were placed in iron lungs

62
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:21,460
to help them breathe, forever trapped

63
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,060
in the humming metal tubes.

64
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Polio survivors could look forward to a life in a world

65
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:34,780
ill-equipped for their special needs, a life of dependency

66
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:36,840
and continual care.

67
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:42,220
And my own family, they were kind of locked in

68
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:44,120
to taking care of me.

69
00:03:44,121 --> 00:03:45,760
I was like the dishes.

70
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:49,420
No, I felt as though I was treated like the dishes.

71
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:52,620
It's your turn to give her her exercises today.

72
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:54,760
I gave her exercises yesterday.

73
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:55,760
You know, let's switch.

74
00:03:55,860 --> 00:03:57,560
I'll do it today if you do it for me tomorrow,

75
00:03:57,660 --> 00:03:59,260
that kind of thing.

76
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,440
Families were ill-equipped as well,

77
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:05,680
and so were important public institutions.

78
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:10,540
In my school, there were many times when I was just forgotten.

79
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:14,120
And I'll never forget the time I was left in the classroom.

80
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:21,600
The teacher hadn't made any arrangement for me, and I sat in the room, this little child,

81
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:27,540
just crying silently because I didn't want to bother anybody.

82
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:33,160
And I was just going to sit there, but no provision had been made for me.

83
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:40,020
Often polio meant a series of long, painful surgeries to free paralyzed limbs.

84
00:04:40,021 --> 00:04:44,060
Most polio patients have gone through several operations.

85
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:45,860
And the thing with the operations

86
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:53,500
was that they rehabilitated you to your optimum at one point.

87
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:57,140
Then they did the operation, and you lost all that ground,

88
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:01,400
and you had to restart all over again.

89
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:02,440
And that was hard.

90
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:03,540
That was really hard.

91
00:05:06,580 --> 00:05:09,380
Even the Egyptians battled the dreaded disease

92
00:05:09,381 --> 00:05:12,020
polio, but an epidemic striking thousands

93
00:05:12,120 --> 00:05:15,320
was a phenomenon of the 20th century.

94
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:18,820
It was in 1916 that the horror had begun.

95
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:22,100
America preparing for World War I had little idea

96
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:25,160
that a devastating and destructive virus was

97
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:26,600
about to attack at home.

98
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:31,740
It was in the muggy heat of Manhattan in June

99
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:34,140
that the first cases were reported.

100
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:37,540
By July, a few cases had grown to 100.

101
00:05:37,541 --> 00:05:42,120
By September, over 1,000 new cases had appeared.

102
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:45,340
Health officials and doctors were baffled.

103
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,280
Why polio?

104
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:48,380
Why now?

105
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:55,860
When the contagion subsided, over 27,000 children

106
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:59,060
had been infected, all in just under five months.

107
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:01,060
Many would recover.

108
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:03,300
Polio wasn't always fatal.

109
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:07,540
But 2,000 were paralyzed, and over 2,000 were paralyzed.

110
00:06:07,541 --> 00:06:10,540
had died, astounding numbers for a disease that

111
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:11,780
was considered rare.

112
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:16,980
From the beginning, the questions mounted

113
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,280
faster than the answers.

114
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:22,620
The little that was known wasn't very encouraging.

115
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:25,660
The major challenge in medicine

116
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:28,760
and in trying to prevent disease and control disease

117
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:32,240
is to find the causes of the disease and how it evolves.

118
00:06:32,340 --> 00:06:34,340
So we did not know what caused polymyelitis,

119
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,100
so we could not prevent the disease.

120
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:42,540
Even if the cause of polio wasn't known,

121
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:46,020
early medical pioneers had worked to discover the secrets

122
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:48,720
of other deadly viruses.

123
00:06:48,820 --> 00:06:53,080
In 1796, Edward Jenner experimented with a killer

124
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:57,800
virus and gave the world its first vaccine.

125
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:00,600
Smallpox was considered a major scorch,

126
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:02,960
had destroyed civilizations.

127
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:07,520
So Edward Jenner was a very, very, very

128
00:07:07,540 --> 00:07:12,740
observed that milkmaids that used to milk the cows

129
00:07:12,741 --> 00:07:14,820
would not get smallpox.

130
00:07:14,821 --> 00:07:17,720
That observation led him to believe

131
00:07:17,721 --> 00:07:20,860
that individuals infected with cowpox

132
00:07:20,861 --> 00:07:23,620
would become immune against smallpox.

133
00:07:23,621 --> 00:07:28,300
So Jenner used the cowpox to protect individuals

134
00:07:28,301 --> 00:07:30,520
against smallpox.

135
00:07:30,521 --> 00:07:32,660
The Latin word for cow is vaca,

136
00:07:32,661 --> 00:07:35,200
so Jenner called his method a vaccination.

137
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:40,080
Louis Pasteur, almost 100 years later,

138
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:41,780
would provide the next vital link

139
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:44,440
in understanding immunization.

140
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:47,620
Working with rabies, Pasteur believed, like Jenner,

141
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:50,820
that the disease itself could be used to fight the infection

142
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:52,960
it caused.

143
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:54,960
But rather than inject the blood directly,

144
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:59,400
this time Pasteur would dilute or attenuate the rabies virus.

145
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:02,100
He reasoned correctly that a weakened virus would still

146
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:03,940
cause the body to fight the infection

147
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:06,540
without causing the illness.

148
00:08:06,541 --> 00:08:10,240
Pasteur diluted rabies in the spinal cord tissue of animals,

149
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:13,180
then ground the tissue and injected it into humans.

150
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:15,980
It was a risky, sometimes deadly procedure.

151
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:19,880
Sometimes it worked, but it was very controversial.

152
00:08:19,980 --> 00:08:22,060
Pester, like many creative scientists,

153
00:08:22,160 --> 00:08:25,120
went against many of the traditions of the day.

154
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:29,300
And so he endured criticism as many creative scientists

155
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:31,700
and people in other fields would.

156
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:33,860
In the 19th century, scientists

157
00:08:33,861 --> 00:08:37,860
worked to solve the polio puzzle, but progress was slow.

158
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:39,640
MANO MARKOVICIALO- Many times, those diseases

159
00:08:39,740 --> 00:08:41,940
were supposed to be coming from fumes,

160
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:44,840
from the environment, you know, and from the positions

161
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:49,040
of the stars, and so on and so forth.

162
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:52,080
One of the major challenges in the prevention of a disease

163
00:08:52,180 --> 00:08:54,740
is to find out what provokes that disease.

164
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:57,880
In 1908, scientist Karl Landensteiner

165
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:00,820
proved what many scientists had suspected.

166
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:03,860
Polio was caused by a virus.

167
00:09:03,861 --> 00:09:05,620
It was the first breakthrough.

168
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:10,640
OK, now we can see if through manipulation of this virus,

169
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:14,100
in a way or another, eventually we can have a vaccine.

170
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:17,400
But knowing that a virus caused polio was one thing.

171
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:21,620
Finding a way to vaccinate against it was another.

172
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:24,180
By the early 1920s, outbreaks of polio

173
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:26,320
were becoming more frequent.

174
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:29,680
Polio dominated the headlines during summer months.

175
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:33,480
And America was about to learn that even the mighty,

176
00:09:33,481 --> 00:09:34,480
weren't immune.

177
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:42,280
In 1796, Edward Jenner tested his theory of immunization

178
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:45,660
by scratching the arm of a young boy with a knife,

179
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:49,660
then rubbing cowpox infected material into the wound.

180
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:53,060
The boy got sick, but recovered, and was then

181
00:09:53,160 --> 00:09:54,400
immune to smallpox.

182
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:01,440
As America danced its way into the 1920s,

183
00:10:01,441 --> 00:10:03,880
the nation's optimism and sense of youthful fun

184
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:06,180
was tempered by a creeping illness that

185
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:08,580
could strike at any moment.

186
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:11,920
Polio was taking more lives and crippling more children

187
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:13,380
every year.

188
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:16,180
Although it preferred children, adults were not immune.

189
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:21,460
Franklin Roosevelt was a young and ambitious star

190
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:23,300
in New York politics.

191
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:26,060
Cousin Teddy had paved the way, and Franklin,

192
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:30,100
patrician, wealthy, was poised to carry on the Roosevelt

193
00:10:30,101 --> 00:10:34,700
But polio would create a detour in his political life,

194
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:39,180
a fortunate accident for the fight against the disease.

195
00:10:39,280 --> 00:10:42,580
In 1920, Franklin had just finished a grueling campaign

196
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:44,040
for vice president.

197
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:48,220
He lost, but was considered a contender for the presidency.

198
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:51,420
At 38, he was poised for greatness.

199
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:54,420
All he had to do was step up to accept the mantle.

200
00:10:54,421 --> 00:10:55,121
the mantle.

201
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:00,620
Tired by his vice presidential campaign,

202
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:04,600
Roosevelt retired to Campobello, his family retreat.

203
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:08,600
On the first day, he swam and went fishing with his children.

204
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:11,940
On the second day, he felt a little feverish.

205
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:15,400
On the morning of the third day, he fell to the floor,

206
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:16,300
unable to get up.

207
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:22,440
Fortunately, a doctor was vacationing nearby,

208
00:11:22,441 --> 00:11:26,080
But the diagnosis was devastating.

209
00:11:26,180 --> 00:11:26,980
It was polio.

210
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:36,020
Roosevelt easily could have retired into a life of luxury.

211
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:38,920
Instead, he chose to fight the disease.

212
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:43,560
Roosevelt was desperately trying to regain the ability to walk.

213
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:46,960
And in 1924, he went down to Warm Springs, Georgia,

214
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:49,340
because he heard that there were warm waters and a pool

215
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:50,600
at the spa there.

216
00:11:50,601 --> 00:11:52,360
He thought that swimming in the warm water

217
00:11:52,460 --> 00:11:56,240
and moving in the warm water would relieve his muscles,

218
00:11:56,340 --> 00:11:59,640
and he could develop them and learn to walk.

219
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:02,540
The warm water soothed his weary muscles,

220
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:04,680
and he found comfort in the company of others

221
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:07,120
who shared his affliction.

222
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:11,020
So enamored was Roosevelt with Warm Springs that in 1927,

223
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:13,620
he decided to buy it.

224
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:16,660
But a polio resort would never draw healthy patrons

225
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:19,720
to waters used by polio patients.

226
00:12:19,721 --> 00:12:23,060
Warm Springs was a therapeutic success.

227
00:12:23,160 --> 00:12:26,200
But as a resort, it was a financial disaster.

228
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:31,900
The next few years, Roosevelt worked in his law office

229
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,100
part time, but spent most of his time searching for a cure,

230
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:39,740
working futilely to recover the strength of his paralyzed limbs.

231
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:45,620
Roosevelt's convalescence was long,

232
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:48,540
but his return to public life was triumphant.

233
00:12:48,541 --> 00:12:51,040
A familiar figure, now on crutches,

234
00:12:51,140 --> 00:12:53,640
slowly mounted the rostrum.

235
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:58,180
He was clearly an heroic figure to the delegates.

236
00:12:58,280 --> 00:13:00,180
Easily elected governor of New York,

237
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:03,880
he had emerged from his trial with conviction and strength,

238
00:13:03,980 --> 00:13:07,300
prepared to lead a country headed for its own dark trials

239
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:08,200
of confidence.

240
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,800
Had Warm Springs been even a modest success,

241
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:16,340
the story of polio might have been different.

242
00:13:16,341 --> 00:13:20,480
But by 1928, Roosevelt had spent almost $200,000,

243
00:13:20,580 --> 00:13:22,840
nearly two-thirds of his family fortune,

244
00:13:22,940 --> 00:13:24,440
trying to keep the resort afloat.

245
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:29,920
The unlikely man who would find the solution

246
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:33,900
was Roosevelt's friend and law partner, Basil O'Connor,

247
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:35,560
the son of Irish immigrants.

248
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:39,400
O'Connor had worked his way up from the ghettos of New York.

249
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:44,260
He was a no-nonsense leader, gruff, but compassionate.

250
00:13:44,261 --> 00:13:49,940
Basil O'Connor was a bulldog once he got onto an idea.

251
00:13:50,040 --> 00:13:52,400
He was tough.

252
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:53,640
He was soft-hearted, too.

253
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:54,900
He didn't like to fire people.

254
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,100
He remembered that his father had once been fired

255
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,480
and saw his father come home crying.

256
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:02,420
So he had a lot of compassion, but he was also

257
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:04,920
a hard-boiled kind of guy.

258
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:07,020
As Roosevelt built his political base,

259
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:10,520
it was O'Connor he turned to to get things done.

260
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:13,160
He liked O'Connor and trusted him.

261
00:14:13,161 --> 00:14:16,260
O'Connor was the logical person to turn to when Warm Springs

262
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:20,140
was on the brink of financial ruin.

263
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:23,200
Basil O'Connor reluctantly went down to Warm Springs

264
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:24,240
with Roosevelt.

265
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:25,740
And Roosevelt said, come on.

266
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:28,780
And he wasn't too enthusiastic about the whole idea,

267
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:32,740
didn't give a darn about polio or polio victims.

268
00:14:32,840 --> 00:14:36,920
But he agreed to do it and gradually got more interested.

269
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:40,160
And eventually, he gave up his law practice

270
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:41,860
and made it a crusade.

271
00:14:41,861 --> 00:14:45,160
As O'Connor thought about what might be done with Warm Springs,

272
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:49,200
Roosevelt's political skills were propelling him forward.

273
00:14:49,300 --> 00:14:52,200
By 1932, Roosevelt had reached the highest office

274
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:53,800
in the land.

275
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:55,780
Gripped by the Great Depression,

276
00:14:55,880 --> 00:15:01,120
America had a president that had experience with hardship.

277
00:15:01,220 --> 00:15:04,480
Roosevelt understood fear and uncertainty,

278
00:15:04,580 --> 00:15:07,680
and he understood the power of the presidency.

279
00:15:07,780 --> 00:15:10,480
Roosevelt was quick to get on the radio

280
00:15:10,481 --> 00:15:13,820
and what he called fireside chats and reassure the country.

281
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:16,620
There was his famous phrase, that we have nothing to fear

282
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:18,220
but fear itself.

283
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:19,920
He sold the country on that.

284
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:23,520
Conditions didn't improve terribly much until World War

285
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:27,860
II and with huge government spending on war production

286
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:29,040
and so on.

287
00:15:29,140 --> 00:15:30,440
That really ended the Depression.

288
00:15:30,540 --> 00:15:34,240
But Roosevelt ended it in people's minds.

289
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,340
Roosevelt's manipulation of the media

290
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:39,240
would help him wage a great battle against the disease

291
00:15:39,241 --> 00:15:41,980
he felt compelled to hide.

292
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:45,540
Mainly, it was just a general gentleman's agreement

293
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,280
among the media and so on that you didn't photograph

294
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:50,280
the president in this way.

295
00:15:50,380 --> 00:15:53,080
So the country as a whole did not realize

296
00:15:53,180 --> 00:15:55,080
that he was crippled.

297
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:58,000
Newspapers, radio, and film were helping America

298
00:15:58,100 --> 00:15:59,700
shape its opinions.

299
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:02,200
And Roosevelt was about to use his political clout

300
00:16:02,300 --> 00:16:05,540
to launch a great crusade against polio.

301
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:08,840
Roosevelt and O'Connor had an idea.

302
00:16:08,841 --> 00:16:11,480
Warm Springs could be saved by turning it

303
00:16:11,580 --> 00:16:14,980
into a nonprofit organization called the National Foundation

304
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:17,580
for Infantile Paralysis.

305
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:20,620
The foundation would provide money to help the afflicted

306
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:23,880
and fund the search for a vaccine.

307
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:27,320
To raise money, Roosevelt sponsored a presidential ball

308
00:16:27,420 --> 00:16:30,520
and invited political appointees from around the country

309
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:32,500
to attend.

310
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:34,500
The birthday balls were a big success

311
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:35,640
all over the country.

312
00:16:35,740 --> 00:16:38,840
They started to raise about $1 million a year.

313
00:16:38,841 --> 00:16:41,940
And they didn't quite know what to do with all that money.

314
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:44,440
So they began to establish local chapters

315
00:16:44,540 --> 00:16:45,640
all over the country.

316
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:48,220
But the foundation's coffers would stay filled

317
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:51,420
only so long as Roosevelt was president.

318
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:54,660
A longer-term solution to the fundraising dilemma was needed.

319
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:06,160
Advertising jingles were beginning to help sell toothpaste.

320
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:08,840
Roosevelt and O'Connor had an idea.

321
00:17:08,841 --> 00:17:11,680
Maybe they could find a way to convince America to find the

322
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:13,920
drive for a polio cure.

323
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:16,240
Hollywood was anxious to help.

324
00:17:16,340 --> 00:17:18,820
All that was needed was a slogan.

325
00:17:18,920 --> 00:17:22,360
So in 1937, the National Foundation

326
00:17:22,460 --> 00:17:25,560
for Infantil Paralysis was formed.

327
00:17:25,660 --> 00:17:27,660
And they had a meeting.

328
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:29,400
And Eddie Cantor was there.

329
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:34,340
And he said, why not have people send dimes to the White House?

330
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:38,840
And maybe we can call it the March of Dimes.

331
00:17:38,841 --> 00:17:39,740
Oh, I know.

332
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:41,780
That's a march of dimes, the infantile paralysis fund.

333
00:17:41,880 --> 00:17:42,840
That's right.

334
00:17:42,940 --> 00:17:44,240
Judy, when I spend a dime on myself

335
00:17:44,340 --> 00:17:45,480
for some little luxury like this,

336
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:47,580
I always think about those unfortunate kids

337
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:50,580
and how far just a dime will go toward helping.

338
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:52,580
At first, Roosevelt and O'Connor thought

339
00:17:52,680 --> 00:17:54,720
their campaign had failed.

340
00:17:54,820 --> 00:17:57,600
Only a few dimes trickled in.

341
00:17:57,700 --> 00:18:00,160
But soon, the trickle became a torrent.

342
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:02,900
So overwhelmed was the mail room at the White House

343
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:07,340
that important correspondents couldn't get through.

344
00:18:07,341 --> 00:18:10,640
The money poured in quickly, but a scientific tragedy

345
00:18:10,740 --> 00:18:13,880
not linked to the foundation showed the need to proceed

346
00:18:13,980 --> 00:18:15,580
slowly.

347
00:18:15,680 --> 00:18:19,780
A couple of scientists developed a inactivated virus polio

348
00:18:19,880 --> 00:18:23,720
vaccine and tried it, and it caused some deaths.

349
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:25,860
The vaccine, there was a weakened polio virus.

350
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:27,360
It wasn't weakened enough.

351
00:18:27,460 --> 00:18:33,300
But this made O'Connor and others see the need for a major

352
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:35,840
research program that would hopefully lead

353
00:18:35,841 --> 00:18:38,240
lead to a successful vaccine.

354
00:18:38,340 --> 00:18:42,840
Two children had died, and three had become paralyzed.

355
00:18:42,940 --> 00:18:45,680
O'Connor acted quickly, establishing a vaccination

356
00:18:45,780 --> 00:18:46,880
committee.

357
00:18:46,980 --> 00:18:49,580
Scientists would be screened and chosen carefully

358
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:53,020
before receiving the endorsement of the foundation.

359
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:56,860
Information would be shared amongst the scientists.

360
00:18:56,960 --> 00:19:00,400
Watching anxiously was a young, virtually unknown doctor

361
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:01,940
named Jonas Salk.

362
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:04,000
He was about to embark on a journey

363
00:19:04,001 --> 00:19:08,440
that would change the course of scientific history.

364
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:11,700
When New York newspapers announced the 1916 polio

365
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:15,580
epidemic, people began to flee from New York City.

366
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:19,420
Outlying towns set up roadblocks to keep the strangers out.

367
00:19:26,060 --> 00:19:28,560
As the 1940s came to a close, America

368
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:31,560
had weathered the devastating trial of World War II.

369
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:37,240
The enemy overseas had been vanquished,

370
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:39,580
but the enemy at home, polio, was still

371
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:42,180
just as destructive as ever.

372
00:19:42,280 --> 00:19:47,240
Almost 300,000 cases had been reported since 1916.

373
00:19:47,340 --> 00:19:50,480
Of those, almost 50,000 had died.

374
00:19:56,560 --> 00:20:00,500
Desperate measures were taken to prevent polio's spread.

375
00:20:00,501 --> 00:20:04,400
Literally tons of DDT are used on this dread disease

376
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:06,940
that attacks our young.

377
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,740
Parents wanted freedom from the fear

378
00:20:08,840 --> 00:20:11,640
that their babies would suddenly be stricken.

379
00:20:11,740 --> 00:20:14,240
But progress on a vaccine was slow.

380
00:20:17,260 --> 00:20:20,160
Scientist Albert Sabin had been working to find the solution

381
00:20:20,260 --> 00:20:22,260
since the mid-30s.

382
00:20:22,360 --> 00:20:25,160
Like Jenner and Pasteur, Sabin believed

383
00:20:25,260 --> 00:20:29,060
that weakening the live virus was the key.

384
00:20:29,061 --> 00:20:32,360
Sabin would one day help eradicate polio,

385
00:20:32,460 --> 00:20:34,700
but his early work led to the mistaken belief

386
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:39,400
that polio virus grew only in spinal tissue.

387
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:41,800
It meant that large quantities of the virus

388
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:45,640
could never be harvested since spinal tissue was unstable.

389
00:20:45,740 --> 00:20:48,340
This was the greatest barrier to a vaccine.

390
00:20:48,440 --> 00:20:51,240
But within a few years, one man, Jonas Salk,

391
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:55,580
would hatch a brilliant plan, one based on solid science,

392
00:20:55,581 --> 00:20:59,080
but born of inspiration and hard work.

393
00:20:59,180 --> 00:21:00,920
The son of Russian Jewish immigrant,

394
00:21:01,020 --> 00:21:04,060
Salk had graduated top in his class at college.

395
00:21:04,160 --> 00:21:08,020
With a loan and $1,000 that his parents had scrapped together,

396
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:11,200
he entered medical school at NYU.

397
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:13,900
Ambitious and talented, Salk could look forward

398
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,140
to a promising and lucrative career as a physician.

399
00:21:20,240 --> 00:21:22,580
Young doctors in training witnessed firsthand

400
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:25,580
the suffering of polio patients, but some

401
00:21:25,581 --> 00:21:28,040
Salk could also hear the hopeful prediction

402
00:21:28,041 --> 00:21:30,820
that a new field, virology, might soon

403
00:21:30,821 --> 00:21:34,020
solve the polio puzzle.

404
00:21:34,021 --> 00:21:37,300
Inspired, Salk opted to enter the research field.

405
00:21:37,301 --> 00:21:39,600
When a professor asked him why he turned down

406
00:21:39,601 --> 00:21:42,360
a lucrative career, Salk replied,

407
00:21:42,361 --> 00:21:44,400
there's more to life than money.

408
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:50,440
Upon graduation, he began looking for a research home

409
00:21:50,441 --> 00:21:52,580
to start his exploration.

410
00:21:52,581 --> 00:21:55,020
He found it at the University of Michigan

411
00:21:55,021 --> 00:21:58,200
with Thomas Francis, who was experimenting with the flu

412
00:21:58,201 --> 00:21:59,120
virus.

413
00:21:59,121 --> 00:22:02,620
Francis was experimenting with a new approach for vaccines.

414
00:22:02,621 --> 00:22:06,360
Instead of weakening the virus as Pasteur and Sabin had done,

415
00:22:06,361 --> 00:22:09,660
he killed the inside of the virus, the part that

416
00:22:09,661 --> 00:22:11,680
allowed it to replicate.

417
00:22:11,681 --> 00:22:14,000
The outer shell could still fool the body

418
00:22:14,001 --> 00:22:19,460
into believing it was infected and thus fight the disease.

419
00:22:19,461 --> 00:22:21,700
Francis's work was made possible in part

420
00:22:21,701 --> 00:22:24,800
because by 1935, the electron microscope

421
00:22:24,801 --> 00:22:29,780
provided the first look at a virus.

422
00:22:29,781 --> 00:22:34,880
A virus is basically a package of genetic information.

423
00:22:34,881 --> 00:22:38,680
You could compare it to an audio tape or a video tape

424
00:22:38,681 --> 00:22:43,560
that carries information for making sound or making pictures.

425
00:22:43,561 --> 00:22:48,960
But without a machine to play it, the tape itself is inert.

426
00:22:48,961 --> 00:22:50,700
Healthy cells in the human body

427
00:22:50,701 --> 00:22:53,200
machine that play the polio tape.

428
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:55,540
The proteins on the outer shell of the virus

429
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:57,340
bind to healthy cells.

430
00:22:57,440 --> 00:23:00,340
The inside of the virus, the RNA or DNA,

431
00:23:00,440 --> 00:23:04,440
allows the virus to mutate or grow inside the healthy cell,

432
00:23:04,540 --> 00:23:06,520
taking over the healthy cell.

433
00:23:06,620 --> 00:23:10,520
The human body provides natural protection called antibodies,

434
00:23:10,620 --> 00:23:13,860
whose purpose is to attack the virus and virus infected cells

435
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:15,260
and kill them.

436
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:17,700
But sometimes the virus is too strong

437
00:23:17,800 --> 00:23:20,700
and the immune system is overwhelmed.

438
00:23:20,701 --> 00:23:22,700
As Salk quietly worked in the lab,

439
00:23:22,800 --> 00:23:25,280
Basil O'Connor was busy building the foundation

440
00:23:25,380 --> 00:23:29,040
into a powerful organization.

441
00:23:29,140 --> 00:23:33,040
As a fashion show sparks the polio drive at the Waldorf,

442
00:23:33,140 --> 00:23:35,680
the models lead the parade of dime donors,

443
00:23:35,780 --> 00:23:39,180
and that iron lung really rings with the silver shower, which

444
00:23:39,280 --> 00:23:42,460
will go far to help infantile paralysis sufferers.

445
00:23:42,560 --> 00:23:44,800
The war years had been tough, but donations

446
00:23:44,900 --> 00:23:48,460
continued to pour in, and the public wanted to know how

447
00:23:48,461 --> 00:23:51,020
how their money was being spent.

448
00:23:51,021 --> 00:23:53,260
Unfortunately, O'Connor and the foundation

449
00:23:53,261 --> 00:23:54,200
had little to report.

450
00:23:57,340 --> 00:24:01,280
In 1946, the discovery that poor children were less likely

451
00:24:01,281 --> 00:24:05,120
to contract polio explained polio's sudden destructiveness

452
00:24:05,121 --> 00:24:06,820
in the 20th century.

453
00:24:06,821 --> 00:24:10,240
When you clean up a country and have modern sanitation,

454
00:24:10,241 --> 00:24:14,680
then the infants and children don't develop immunity.

455
00:24:14,681 --> 00:24:18,180
And bango, at some point, the virus strikes.

456
00:24:24,620 --> 00:24:29,160
By 1947, Salk was anxious for his own lab.

457
00:24:29,161 --> 00:24:32,040
He decided on the University of Pittsburgh,

458
00:24:32,041 --> 00:24:35,560
choosing a place where he could explore his own scientific

459
00:24:35,561 --> 00:24:37,260
passions.

460
00:24:37,261 --> 00:24:39,600
Salk went to the University of Pittsburgh

461
00:24:39,601 --> 00:24:42,680
because he said there was nothing there in the way

462
00:24:42,681 --> 00:24:44,280
of virology.

463
00:24:44,281 --> 00:24:47,220
He said going to Pittsburgh was like going west

464
00:24:47,221 --> 00:24:48,620
to the frontier days.

465
00:24:48,621 --> 00:24:50,680
Pittsburgh was the frontier.

466
00:24:50,681 --> 00:24:53,220
And he established his own virus laboratory

467
00:24:53,221 --> 00:24:55,620
where he could be in charge.

468
00:24:55,621 --> 00:24:59,360
At Pittsburgh, Salk lost no time getting to work.

469
00:24:59,361 --> 00:25:02,600
Once he had put together his lab virtually from scratch,

470
00:25:02,601 --> 00:25:05,340
he set about the difficult task of raising money

471
00:25:05,341 --> 00:25:07,100
for his new project.

472
00:25:07,101 --> 00:25:10,040
It was the foundation to which he turned.

473
00:25:10,041 --> 00:25:11,800
It was known that there was a polio virus,

474
00:25:11,801 --> 00:25:15,800
but nobody knew how many types there were or what they did.

475
00:25:15,801 --> 00:25:19,080
And the National Foundation commissioned Salk

476
00:25:19,081 --> 00:25:22,300
to work on typing the virus.

477
00:25:22,301 --> 00:25:24,880
It's important to identify the different strains

478
00:25:24,881 --> 00:25:27,500
of the virus, because the immune response of the body

479
00:25:27,501 --> 00:25:31,180
can be different against different strains.

480
00:25:31,181 --> 00:25:33,880
In the late 40s, scientists working at Harvard

481
00:25:33,881 --> 00:25:37,280
changed the face of polio research.

482
00:25:37,281 --> 00:25:39,980
The highly respected virologist John Enders

483
00:25:39,981 --> 00:25:44,480
brought down the one great barrier to a vaccine.

484
00:25:44,481 --> 00:25:47,180
Anders and his team were cultivating mumps virus

485
00:25:47,181 --> 00:25:48,860
in tissue cultures.

486
00:25:48,861 --> 00:25:50,960
They decided to add a strain of polio

487
00:25:50,961 --> 00:25:53,320
to some leftover cultures.

488
00:25:53,321 --> 00:25:56,000
The results were astonishing.

489
00:25:56,001 --> 00:25:59,100
Not only did the virus grow, it thrived.

490
00:25:59,101 --> 00:26:02,300
In one experiment, it was proven that polio could, in fact,

491
00:26:02,301 --> 00:26:06,400
be grown in non-nervous cell tissue.

492
00:26:06,401 --> 00:26:08,900
This was the plow that broke the plains.

493
00:26:08,901 --> 00:26:13,580
When Salk heard of Ender's 1949 work,

494
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:17,680
he immediately saw that a vaccine would be possible,

495
00:26:17,780 --> 00:26:20,380
and he got ready to work on one.

496
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:22,480
The question asked was which was better,

497
00:26:22,580 --> 00:26:24,720
a weakened vaccine as Sabin favored,

498
00:26:24,820 --> 00:26:29,060
or a killed vaccine as Salk advocated?

499
00:26:29,160 --> 00:26:31,860
Salk would need to convince O'Connor and the Foundation

500
00:26:31,960 --> 00:26:36,060
to support him, and in 1951, he got his chance,

501
00:26:36,061 --> 00:26:38,960
an invitation to address other working scientists

502
00:26:38,961 --> 00:26:41,060
at a conference in Copenhagen.

503
00:26:41,061 --> 00:26:44,500
But it was the trip home that proved almost as valuable

504
00:26:44,501 --> 00:26:45,740
as the conference.

505
00:26:45,741 --> 00:26:48,600
Salk returned to America aboard the Queen Mary.

506
00:26:48,601 --> 00:26:52,200
Also aboard were Basil O'Connor and his wife.

507
00:26:52,201 --> 00:26:55,840
O'Connor invited them to sit at his table.

508
00:26:55,841 --> 00:26:59,820
And Salk was somewhat entranced by this smooth,

509
00:26:59,821 --> 00:27:01,960
sophisticated guy, O'Connor.

510
00:27:01,961 --> 00:27:05,420
And O'Connor was taken with this young, enthusiastic,

511
00:27:05,421 --> 00:27:09,820
serious kid who believed that a vaccine was possible.

512
00:27:09,920 --> 00:27:12,060
It was the beginning of a beautiful friendship.

513
00:27:12,160 --> 00:27:15,660
But what Salk was proposing was not free from risk.

514
00:27:15,760 --> 00:27:18,960
O'Connor still remembered 1935.

515
00:27:19,060 --> 00:27:21,140
The last time a killed virus had been used

516
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:23,900
with devastating results.

517
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:27,680
Salk relied on formalin to kill the inner core of the virus.

518
00:27:27,780 --> 00:27:30,580
It was the exact same method that had been used during the ill

519
00:27:30,680 --> 00:27:33,420
fated experiment in 1935.

520
00:27:33,421 --> 00:27:36,060
This time, however, Salk believed he knew how to kill

521
00:27:36,160 --> 00:27:39,400
the virus completely.

522
00:27:39,500 --> 00:27:42,360
Despite the risk, O'Connor trusted Salk.

523
00:27:42,460 --> 00:27:46,240
The two men, as different as they seemed, had much in common.

524
00:27:46,340 --> 00:27:50,200
Both were sons of immigrants, self-made, hardworking,

525
00:27:50,300 --> 00:27:53,300
determined, and they shared a common passion

526
00:27:53,400 --> 00:27:56,180
to rid the world of polio.

527
00:27:56,280 --> 00:27:58,740
While O'Connor was interested in Salk and his work,

528
00:27:58,840 --> 00:28:00,680
there were other scientists competing

529
00:28:00,780 --> 00:28:02,480
for the foundation's funds.

530
00:28:02,481 --> 00:28:05,140
The enormous expense of testing a vaccine meant

531
00:28:05,141 --> 00:28:07,880
the foundation would have to be careful about backing

532
00:28:07,881 --> 00:28:11,020
one scientist or approach prematurely.

533
00:28:11,021 --> 00:28:14,160
A recent scandal had proven the point.

534
00:28:14,161 --> 00:28:18,160
Noted scientist Hilary Koprowski shocked the scientific community

535
00:28:18,161 --> 00:28:23,460
in 1951, announcing that he had on his own injected live virus

536
00:28:23,461 --> 00:28:25,200
into volunteers.

537
00:28:25,201 --> 00:28:26,840
Doubly shocking was the revelation

538
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:28,740
that the volunteers had been patients

539
00:28:28,741 --> 00:28:31,340
at a New York mental institution.

540
00:28:31,341 --> 00:28:35,620
No one was infected, but the foundation was outraged.

541
00:28:35,621 --> 00:28:38,320
Salk was certain his killed vaccine was the key,

542
00:28:38,321 --> 00:28:40,320
but important work remained in the lab

543
00:28:40,321 --> 00:28:43,280
to prove the vaccine was ready for the public.

544
00:28:43,281 --> 00:28:46,620
Salk focused on the strongest, most virulent strain of polio,

545
00:28:46,621 --> 00:28:48,380
the Mahoney strain.

546
00:28:48,381 --> 00:28:50,460
He reasoned that since the virus was dead,

547
00:28:50,461 --> 00:28:52,100
strength wouldn't matter.

548
00:28:52,101 --> 00:28:55,700
Besides, the strong strain grew well in the laboratory.

549
00:28:55,701 --> 00:28:59,700
The antibody response would be the same in weaker strains.

550
00:28:59,701 --> 00:29:03,440
was making excellent progress, and it was a good thing,

551
00:29:03,540 --> 00:29:06,340
because in 1952, the US was experiencing

552
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:08,840
its worst epidemic yet.

553
00:29:08,940 --> 00:29:13,480
56,000 cases of poliomyelitis, one severe case

554
00:29:13,580 --> 00:29:15,380
for every 3,000 people.

555
00:29:15,480 --> 00:29:18,380
The public cried desperately for a solution.

556
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:23,020
Jonas Salk believed that he could provide it.

557
00:29:23,120 --> 00:29:26,900
In Brooklyn, authorities rounded up 215 cats,

558
00:29:26,901 --> 00:29:30,140
because it was suspected that they might carry polio.

559
00:29:33,040 --> 00:29:36,140
By the 1950s, polio's grip over America

560
00:29:36,240 --> 00:29:38,340
was the strongest it had ever been.

561
00:29:38,440 --> 00:29:41,440
Polio had become a summer ritual of fear.

562
00:29:41,540 --> 00:29:44,120
But Jonas Salk was about to change that.

563
00:29:44,220 --> 00:29:47,460
For five years, Salk had been working feverishly in the lab,

564
00:29:47,560 --> 00:29:51,360
conducting tests to prove that his vaccine could work.

565
00:29:51,460 --> 00:29:54,100
For those beyond a cure, the March of Dimes

566
00:29:54,101 --> 00:29:56,600
did what could be done to ease the pain.

567
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:59,500
Polio was an expensive disease, and many

568
00:29:59,600 --> 00:30:03,240
turned to the March of Dimes for help.

569
00:30:03,340 --> 00:30:09,920
Back in the 50s, my shoes cost between $75 and $100.

570
00:30:10,020 --> 00:30:12,160
My mother didn't have to purchase the brace for me

571
00:30:12,260 --> 00:30:15,860
because she petitioned the March of Dimes,

572
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:22,200
and the March of Dimes provided the money for the brace.

573
00:30:22,201 --> 00:30:24,860
But she had to pay for the shoes.

574
00:30:24,960 --> 00:30:29,240
Rehabilitation required money and patience.

575
00:30:29,340 --> 00:30:33,640
The children and I were told how to walk.

576
00:30:33,740 --> 00:30:39,860
And it was the right arm, left foot, left arm, right foot.

577
00:30:39,960 --> 00:30:42,280
And you know, you had to have this rhythm going when you

578
00:30:42,380 --> 00:30:43,480
walked.

579
00:30:43,580 --> 00:30:45,720
You couldn't just walk any kind of way.

580
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:48,560
With each epidemic, the number of polio patients

581
00:30:48,561 --> 00:30:52,060
requiring expensive medical treatment increased.

582
00:30:52,160 --> 00:30:55,960
It seemed that there was no end in sight to the tragedy.

583
00:30:56,060 --> 00:30:58,340
After five long years working in the lab,

584
00:30:58,440 --> 00:31:01,300
Salk was ready for the next momentous step,

585
00:31:01,400 --> 00:31:04,940
trying the vaccine on a human being.

586
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:08,080
In a bold move, he injected not only himself,

587
00:31:08,180 --> 00:31:13,660
but his entire family with his killed polio virus.

588
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:16,880
I think he was confident about the safety of the vaccine,

589
00:31:16,881 --> 00:31:19,980
and he was willing to inoculate himself and his children

590
00:31:20,080 --> 00:31:23,980
as an indication of his confidence in the safety of it.

591
00:31:24,080 --> 00:31:25,760
The injections worked.

592
00:31:25,860 --> 00:31:28,500
Salk and his family showed evidence of antibodies

593
00:31:28,600 --> 00:31:30,260
that could fight polio.

594
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:32,600
But it was far from the proof needed to convince

595
00:31:32,700 --> 00:31:34,800
the scientific community.

596
00:31:34,900 --> 00:31:38,640
For that, a large trial was necessary.

597
00:31:38,740 --> 00:31:41,780
First, Salk decided to conduct a top secret test

598
00:31:41,880 --> 00:31:44,500
on a small number of polio patients at the Watson

599
00:31:44,501 --> 00:31:46,500
for crippled children.

600
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:48,980
Despite his confidence in the vaccine,

601
00:31:49,080 --> 00:31:53,780
Salk had to take every precaution possible.

602
00:31:53,880 --> 00:31:57,040
DR. DR. Salk felt that it would be more acceptable

603
00:31:57,140 --> 00:32:02,180
to have a vaccine used in people who had already been exposed

604
00:32:02,280 --> 00:32:05,720
to the virus than it would be to use that vaccine in people

605
00:32:05,820 --> 00:32:07,620
who had not been exposed.

606
00:32:07,720 --> 00:32:11,060
When the results were in, antibodies had increased.

607
00:32:11,160 --> 00:32:13,800
The experiment had worked.

608
00:32:13,801 --> 00:32:16,840
But would the vaccine work on healthy children?

609
00:32:16,940 --> 00:32:20,300
Would it provide significant protection?

610
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:23,340
Could healthy kids become infected?

611
00:32:23,440 --> 00:32:25,580
These were all possibilities.

612
00:32:25,680 --> 00:32:31,020
Some scientists didn't think the larger trial should proceed.

613
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:34,920
There's a lot of debate about when vaccines would be tried

614
00:32:35,020 --> 00:32:37,320
and who would take them.

615
00:32:37,420 --> 00:32:41,520
There's a consideration of cost and who should pay for the

616
00:32:41,521 --> 00:32:44,960
who should be given the vaccine, how broadly it should be used,

617
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:46,360
and so on.

618
00:32:46,460 --> 00:32:48,320
But Salk, with O'Connor's support,

619
00:32:48,420 --> 00:32:50,600
called for an immediate trial.

620
00:32:50,700 --> 00:32:53,760
There was no time to waste.

621
00:32:53,860 --> 00:32:56,140
Any vaccine that is available should be used.

622
00:32:56,240 --> 00:32:59,580
And whatever vaccine is available should be used now

623
00:32:59,680 --> 00:33:02,080
and should be used before this coming summer.

624
00:33:02,180 --> 00:33:03,240
The idea...

625
00:33:03,340 --> 00:33:05,440
For a large trial, hundreds of thousands of people

626
00:33:05,540 --> 00:33:07,840
would need to be convinced to participate.

627
00:33:07,940 --> 00:33:10,720
Parents would be volunteering their children.

628
00:33:10,721 --> 00:33:14,820
Salk found himself playing a new role now, public spokesperson

629
00:33:14,920 --> 00:33:16,860
for his vaccine.

630
00:33:16,960 --> 00:33:18,360
And the press loved him.

631
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:21,460
Jonas Salk was direct, sincere, and intelligent.

632
00:33:21,560 --> 00:33:25,100
He inspired confidence and trust, the perfect hero

633
00:33:25,200 --> 00:33:28,200
to soothe an anxious, expectant public.

634
00:33:28,300 --> 00:33:32,880
But Salk embraced his growing fame with great reluctance.

635
00:33:32,980 --> 00:33:36,240
Some scientists were outraged at the increased attention

636
00:33:36,340 --> 00:33:37,480
paid to Salk.

637
00:33:37,481 --> 00:33:40,380
His willingness to mention the contribution of others

638
00:33:40,480 --> 00:33:43,920
did little to mollify the scientific community.

639
00:33:44,020 --> 00:33:47,120
Salk later said that Enders threw a forward pass,

640
00:33:47,220 --> 00:33:48,920
and I caught it.

641
00:33:49,020 --> 00:33:51,400
And other scientists never forgave Salk

642
00:33:51,500 --> 00:33:53,660
for taking that much credit.

643
00:33:53,760 --> 00:33:56,000
Despite the heavy and growing responsibilities

644
00:33:56,100 --> 00:33:58,600
in public, Salk worked privately in the lab

645
00:33:58,700 --> 00:34:02,900
to ensure all details were in place.

646
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:05,200
The last most important hurdle was

647
00:34:05,201 --> 00:34:07,740
was to ensure mass production of his vaccine

648
00:34:07,840 --> 00:34:09,600
was done properly.

649
00:34:09,700 --> 00:34:12,740
Big commercial labs would manufacture large quantities

650
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:15,980
of the vaccine in assembly line fashion.

651
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:18,480
But the slightest mistake could be devastating.

652
00:34:18,580 --> 00:34:22,020
A bad vaccine going out would be disastrous.

653
00:34:22,120 --> 00:34:23,860
Production had to be perfect.

654
00:34:27,620 --> 00:34:30,860
Basil O'Connor was also taking steps to ensure the trial

655
00:34:30,960 --> 00:34:32,400
came off perfectly.

656
00:34:32,500 --> 00:34:34,760
There could be no questions about methodology

657
00:34:34,761 --> 00:34:36,300
or results.

658
00:34:36,400 --> 00:34:39,400
The foundation would provide the logistical support

659
00:34:39,500 --> 00:34:41,800
and coordinate personnel.

660
00:34:41,900 --> 00:34:44,500
Through the early spring of 53, the foundation

661
00:34:44,600 --> 00:34:47,640
struggled with numerous decisions.

662
00:34:47,740 --> 00:34:51,580
Over 500,000 people would be participating.

663
00:34:51,680 --> 00:34:54,980
Boxes of supplies and vial after vial of vaccine

664
00:34:55,080 --> 00:34:57,020
would have to be distributed.

665
00:34:57,120 --> 00:34:59,520
Then there was the task of tracking and carefully

666
00:34:59,620 --> 00:35:02,380
tabulating the results.

667
00:35:02,381 --> 00:35:05,480
Thomas Francis, Salk's friend and mentor from Ann Arbor,

668
00:35:05,580 --> 00:35:08,580
was asked to oversee the trial.

669
00:35:08,680 --> 00:35:11,320
It was a fitting reunion with the man who had helped

670
00:35:11,420 --> 00:35:14,360
inspire the Salk vaccine.

671
00:35:14,460 --> 00:35:18,200
By early 1954, two years after Salk had injected himself

672
00:35:18,300 --> 00:35:20,900
with the vaccine, the vaccine was ready to be given

673
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:22,300
to the public.

674
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:24,740
Salk and O'Connor were convinced the vaccinations

675
00:35:24,840 --> 00:35:27,480
would proceed safely, but Sabin still

676
00:35:27,580 --> 00:35:29,180
doubted the wisdom of the trial.

677
00:35:29,181 --> 00:35:34,520
There had never been a trial of the size of the 1954 field

678
00:35:34,620 --> 00:35:37,420
trial of the Salk vaccine.

679
00:35:37,520 --> 00:35:42,860
And before that trial was approved by the Public Health

680
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:49,300
Service, many bitterly opposed it, among them Enders, Sabin.

681
00:35:49,400 --> 00:35:50,440
They thought it was dangerous.

682
00:35:50,540 --> 00:35:53,040
They thought, A, it wouldn't work, and B, it was dangerous.

683
00:35:53,140 --> 00:35:55,740
Enders told Salk that he was unethical.

684
00:35:55,840 --> 00:35:58,640
Salk and the foundation believe they had to try.

685
00:35:58,641 --> 00:36:03,380
On April 26, 1954, the trials began.

686
00:36:03,480 --> 00:36:07,040
Children were inoculated by thousands of volunteer nurses

687
00:36:07,140 --> 00:36:08,180
and doctors.

688
00:36:08,280 --> 00:36:12,120
They were called polio pioneers and told by teachers

689
00:36:12,220 --> 00:36:16,320
that pioneers are special people who go first.

690
00:36:16,420 --> 00:36:20,160
Chalkboards across America were filled with the same message.

691
00:36:23,200 --> 00:36:26,940
Over 200,000 vials of the vaccine would be needed.

692
00:36:26,941 --> 00:36:32,040
needed, 1 million needles, as many cotton swabs, not to mention

693
00:36:32,140 --> 00:36:36,120
the public space required for such an undertaking.

694
00:36:36,220 --> 00:36:39,020
First, blood was taken from each child,

695
00:36:39,120 --> 00:36:41,380
then the inoculation given.

696
00:36:41,480 --> 00:36:43,520
Two weeks later, another blood sample

697
00:36:43,620 --> 00:36:48,200
was taken to compare the difference in antibody levels.

698
00:36:48,300 --> 00:36:51,200
All the samples had to be checked carefully.

699
00:36:51,300 --> 00:36:56,940
With over 500,000 participants, the logistics were enormous.

700
00:36:56,941 --> 00:36:59,080
At first, everything went well.

701
00:36:59,180 --> 00:37:01,320
Then the unimaginable happened.

702
00:37:01,420 --> 00:37:04,020
A child inoculated by the Salk vaccine

703
00:37:04,120 --> 00:37:06,020
had come down with polio.

704
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:08,920
It was a tragedy that could end the trials.

705
00:37:09,020 --> 00:37:11,520
Even worse, the media got hold of the news,

706
00:37:11,620 --> 00:37:16,060
releasing it to a startled, worried public.

707
00:37:16,160 --> 00:37:17,560
Salk was crushed.

708
00:37:17,660 --> 00:37:21,140
O'Connor told friends later, Salk was heartbroken.

709
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:24,140
It was his vaccine, and he knew there wasn't a damn thing

710
00:37:24,141 --> 00:37:27,040
wrong with it when it was made properly.

711
00:37:27,140 --> 00:37:29,940
Fortunately, the disaster was soon explained.

712
00:37:30,040 --> 00:37:33,820
One of the labs had failed to kill the virus completely.

713
00:37:33,920 --> 00:37:37,420
Salk reassured the public that it would never happen again.

714
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:40,360
Our contention in the thing that we discussed today

715
00:37:40,460 --> 00:37:43,760
has been that it should be possible to prevent

716
00:37:43,860 --> 00:37:47,660
paralytic poliomyelitis by the proper use of a properly

717
00:37:47,760 --> 00:37:49,800
constituted vaccine.

718
00:37:49,900 --> 00:37:51,700
Despite their fears, most parents

719
00:37:51,800 --> 00:37:53,540
were desperate for a cure.

720
00:37:53,541 --> 00:37:55,780
and they agreed to continue the trial.

721
00:37:55,880 --> 00:38:00,640
Most believed that the benefits outweighed the risk.

722
00:38:00,740 --> 00:38:03,680
In the scientific community, and particularly in medicine,

723
00:38:03,780 --> 00:38:05,920
people like to move slowly.

724
00:38:06,020 --> 00:38:08,520
You know, there's a saying, first do no harm.

725
00:38:08,620 --> 00:38:10,160
That isn't really possible.

726
00:38:10,260 --> 00:38:13,060
You can't do anything without doing some harm.

727
00:38:13,160 --> 00:38:16,700
By March of 1955, all the lab work had been completed

728
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:18,500
and the results tabulated.

729
00:38:18,600 --> 00:38:22,840
An anxious and expectant nation waited for the results.

730
00:38:22,841 --> 00:38:26,440
Conference was called on April 12, 1955,

731
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:29,740
coincidentally the 10th anniversary of Franklin

732
00:38:29,840 --> 00:38:31,840
Roosevelt's death.

733
00:38:31,940 --> 00:38:36,280
I was a reporter on the Minneapolis Tribune in 1955.

734
00:38:36,380 --> 00:38:39,320
We had had several bad polio epidemics.

735
00:38:39,420 --> 00:38:41,260
We were frantic for this news.

736
00:38:41,360 --> 00:38:43,720
And the PR people and the guards

737
00:38:43,820 --> 00:38:47,660
brought the stacks of press releases into the press room.

738
00:38:47,760 --> 00:38:50,100
And we all surged forward to get them,

739
00:38:50,101 --> 00:38:53,640
like a bunch of shoppers at Macy's during a sale,

740
00:38:53,740 --> 00:38:55,100
every man for himself.

741
00:38:55,200 --> 00:38:57,340
Soon, the word was out.

742
00:38:57,440 --> 00:38:59,280
The Salk vaccine worked.

743
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:01,940
A nation gripped by fear summer after summer

744
00:39:02,040 --> 00:39:05,320
was able to breathe a heavy, happy sigh of relief

745
00:39:05,420 --> 00:39:07,580
as Salk shared the news.

746
00:39:07,680 --> 00:39:10,920
Asked by the media who owned the patent to the vaccine,

747
00:39:11,020 --> 00:39:13,360
Salk replied, the people do.

748
00:39:13,460 --> 00:39:15,020
There is no patent.

749
00:39:15,120 --> 00:39:17,200
Could you patent the sun?

750
00:39:17,201 --> 00:39:20,500
Salk's vaccine proved that polio could be beaten,

751
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:23,140
but the task to end the scourge in America

752
00:39:23,240 --> 00:39:25,340
was far from accomplished.

753
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:28,640
As news of the Salk vaccine was reaching a hungry, anxious

754
00:39:28,740 --> 00:39:32,620
public, Albert Sabin was working on his own vaccine.

755
00:39:32,720 --> 00:39:36,560
Sabin remained convinced that a live virus approach was better

756
00:39:36,660 --> 00:39:38,560
than the Salk vaccine.

757
00:39:38,660 --> 00:39:42,600
His work was about to profoundly influence the challenge

758
00:39:42,700 --> 00:39:47,200
to eradicate polio from the world.

759
00:39:47,201 --> 00:39:51,580
In the 10 years from 1952 to 1962,

760
00:39:51,680 --> 00:39:54,200
the yearly number of reported polio cases

761
00:39:54,300 --> 00:39:58,080
went from 56,000 to 910.

762
00:40:01,520 --> 00:40:05,060
By 1957, millions of children had been vaccinated

763
00:40:05,160 --> 00:40:07,380
with Salk's vaccine for polio.

764
00:40:07,480 --> 00:40:09,560
It was clear that although Salk's vaccine

765
00:40:09,660 --> 00:40:14,360
dealt polio a lethal blow, it couldn't finish the job.

766
00:40:14,460 --> 00:40:16,700
His vaccine required multiple shots

767
00:40:16,701 --> 00:40:18,900
to maintain its effectiveness.

768
00:40:19,000 --> 00:40:21,200
Unbelievably, some had to be convinced

769
00:40:21,300 --> 00:40:24,700
to take the follow-up shots that were required.

770
00:40:24,800 --> 00:40:28,940
Albert Sabin believed he had found a solution to the problem.

771
00:40:29,040 --> 00:40:31,640
He had successfully weakened the virus,

772
00:40:31,740 --> 00:40:34,160
and he was convinced his live virus method

773
00:40:34,260 --> 00:40:36,660
was better than Salk's.

774
00:40:36,760 --> 00:40:40,900
It could be taken orally and was therefore much more convenient.

775
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:43,000
Although it required refrigeration,

776
00:40:43,001 --> 00:40:46,940
it was much easier to administer on a mass basis.

777
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:49,280
No matter where people live, they could easily

778
00:40:49,380 --> 00:40:51,140
take the vaccine.

779
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:52,980
Those giving the Sabin vaccine needed

780
00:40:53,080 --> 00:40:55,620
no special medical training.

781
00:40:55,720 --> 00:40:59,080
Best of all, children were grateful to be spared

782
00:40:59,180 --> 00:41:02,320
the nasty needle.

783
00:41:02,420 --> 00:41:07,060
In the late 1950s, in an unusual example of detente,

784
00:41:07,160 --> 00:41:09,720
the Sabin vaccine was sent to the Soviet Union

785
00:41:09,820 --> 00:41:12,700
and Eastern Europe and given to hundreds of thousands

786
00:41:12,701 --> 00:41:16,100
of children, ostensibly used to test the effectiveness

787
00:41:16,200 --> 00:41:19,180
of Sabin's vaccine, the Eastern European trials

788
00:41:19,280 --> 00:41:21,940
were so successful, the Sabin approach

789
00:41:22,040 --> 00:41:25,220
became the vaccine of choice the world over.

790
00:41:25,320 --> 00:41:28,220
In fact, it was Sabin's vaccine that would ultimately

791
00:41:28,320 --> 00:41:31,320
eradicate polio in the Western Hemisphere,

792
00:41:31,420 --> 00:41:33,480
although it would take decades to do so.

793
00:41:36,520 --> 00:41:41,120
The last reported case was in Peru as recently as 1992.

794
00:41:41,121 --> 00:41:43,460
Today, polio continues to cripple children

795
00:41:43,560 --> 00:41:45,060
in developing nations.

796
00:41:45,160 --> 00:41:47,060
But with the help of Rotary International,

797
00:41:47,160 --> 00:41:48,920
the Pan American Health Organization

798
00:41:49,020 --> 00:41:52,260
plans to eradicate polio from the world.

799
00:41:52,360 --> 00:41:55,300
Despite the tremendous success of those who work to battle

800
00:41:55,400 --> 00:41:58,880
the disease, the fight continues to this day.

801
00:41:58,980 --> 00:42:02,280
Those stricken with polio as children find that as adults,

802
00:42:02,380 --> 00:42:07,280
the disease often returns to cripple again.

803
00:42:07,380 --> 00:42:10,580
Scientists are still trying to discover why those who

804
00:42:10,581 --> 00:42:15,280
who suffered as children suffer again in later years as adults.

805
00:42:15,380 --> 00:42:18,720
Jonas Salk had helped eradicate a cruel disease.

806
00:42:18,820 --> 00:42:21,520
He was still a young man with many years of work

807
00:42:21,620 --> 00:42:23,520
and research ahead of him.

808
00:42:23,620 --> 00:42:26,760
With the help of the March of Dimes in the city of San Diego,

809
00:42:26,860 --> 00:42:29,260
Salk established a research institute

810
00:42:29,360 --> 00:42:32,840
with a profound and unique mission,

811
00:42:32,940 --> 00:42:37,500
a mission inspired by the work he had done to fight polio.

812
00:42:37,501 --> 00:42:41,980
Dr. Salk had a broad view of his place in human history

813
00:42:42,080 --> 00:42:43,480
and human culture.

814
00:42:43,580 --> 00:42:46,440
He felt that as human beings, we should

815
00:42:46,540 --> 00:42:50,780
be conscious of the many dimensions of our existence.

816
00:42:50,880 --> 00:42:53,180
So he founded an institute that would be devoted

817
00:42:53,280 --> 00:42:57,320
to understanding the physical, the mental, and the spiritual

818
00:42:57,420 --> 00:42:59,680
aspects of human beings.

819
00:42:59,780 --> 00:43:04,060
To the very end, Salk approached his work with humility.

820
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:06,060
He spent his last year struggling

821
00:43:06,061 --> 00:43:09,900
to uncover the secrets of another virus that causes terror,

822
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:11,640
the AIDS virus.

823
00:43:11,740 --> 00:43:15,540
Albert Sabin had worked for over four decades to beat polio

824
00:43:15,640 --> 00:43:19,540
and could also claim credit for victory over the disease.

825
00:43:19,640 --> 00:43:21,840
He and Salk's scientific adversaries

826
00:43:21,940 --> 00:43:25,280
had both succeeded in their common goal.

827
00:43:25,380 --> 00:43:27,840
The fear today is of killer viruses

828
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:31,420
that threaten to wipe out entire populations.

829
00:43:31,520 --> 00:43:34,260
The pioneers who worked to unlock the secrets of one

830
00:43:34,261 --> 00:43:38,760
of one of the 20th century's most destructive viruses, polio,

831
00:43:38,761 --> 00:43:42,500
have helped to pave the way for future exploration.

832
00:43:42,501 --> 00:43:44,720
Hopefully, the painful lessons of yesterday

833
00:43:44,721 --> 00:43:49,220
will help answer the difficult questions of tomorrow.

